// The field twin of `pub_on_trait_declaration_rejected`: on a value `struct`'s field, `pub` is
// refused (E0077). A struct's fields are public by construction — it is the value kind and has no
// private state to opt out of — so the word restates what `struct` already said, and worse, it
// would suggest the fields written without it are private, which is the one thing it must not
// imply. Refused rather than accepted-and-ignored, on the same ground `static` is refused where a
// body already decides: a modifier that means nothing is still a modifier a reader has to interpret.
//
// It PARSES — `pub` is part of the field production — so this is a diagnostic with a span on the
// field's NAME, not a parse fumble about an unexpected token. `mut` beside it is untouched: that
// modifier decides something (opt-in mutability), and the two are judged independently.
//
// The `pub` on the METHOD in the same body is legal and means what it says (method-visibility
// arc). That neighbour, one line away, is why a redundant `pub` on a field stopped being harmless.
// expect: error E0077 at 18:9
// expect: error E0077 at 20:13
// expect: exit 1
struct Counter {
    pub label: string
    mut count: int
    pub mut total: int

    pub fn next(): int { return self.count + 1; }
}

echo "unreached";
